Level 1: Classes, encapsulation, generics
1. Build your own DynamicArray<T>
Implement add, get(index), remove(index), size, and contains. Back it with a private Object[] that doubles in size when full.
OOP: encapsulation (keep the array private and expose only safe methods), generics, throwing IndexOutOfBoundsException.
DSA: amortized O(1) append. Work out why doubling gives amortized O(1) while growing by +10 each time gives O(n).
Stretch: shrink the array when it falls to ¼ full. Why ¼ and not ½?


2. Stack and Queue behind interfaces
Define interface Stack<T> with push, pop, peek, isEmpty. Write two implementations: ArrayStack<T>, which reuses problem 1, and LinkedStack<T>. Do the same for Queue<T>, using a circular array.
Apply it: a balanced brackets checker ("{[()]}" is valid, "([)]" is not), and evaluation of postfix expressions like "3 4 + 2 *".
OOP: programming to an interface. Your client code shouldn’t know or care which implementation it’s using.
DSA: LIFO/FIFO and the trade-offs between array-based and linked implementations.
3. Doubly linked list with an Iterator
LinkedList<T> implements Iterable<T>, with a private static nested Node<T> class. Support addFirst, addLast, remove(T), and reverse().
OOP: nested/inner classes and implementing Java’s Iterator contract so that for (T x : list) works.
DSA: pointer manipulation, in-place reversal, and Floyd’s cycle detection (add a deliberately broken createCycle() method for testing).
Level 2: Inheritance, polymorphism, abstraction
4. Shape hierarchy and sorting
abstract class Shape with abstract double area(), plus Circle, Rectangle, and Triangle subclasses. Make Shape implements Comparable<Shape> (compare by area). Then sort a list by perimeter using a Comparator without changing the class.
OOP: abstract classes, method overriding, Comparable vs Comparator, and why equals/hashCode must be overridden together.
DSA: write a generic mergeSort(List<T> list, Comparator<T> c) yourself instead of calling Collections.sort.
5. Pluggable sorting framework
interface SortStrategy<T> with implementations BubbleSort, InsertionSort, MergeSort, QuickSort, and HeapSort. A Sorter class takes a strategy, counts comparisons and swaps, and times each run.
Benchmark on random, already-sorted, and reverse-sorted arrays of size 1K, 10K, and 100K.
OOP: the Strategy pattern and composition over inheritance.
DSA: see best, average, and worst cases empirically. In particular, watch naive QuickSort degrade to O(n²) on sorted input, then fix it with a randomized pivot.
6. Binary Search Tree, then an AVL tree via inheritance
BST<T extends Comparable<T>> with insert, delete (handle all 3 cases), search, height, and inorder/preorder/postorder/level-order traversals.
Then write AVLTree<T> extends BST<T> that overrides insert/delete to rebalance with rotations.
OOP: bounded generics, inheritance, protected vs private, and when overriding gets awkward (a useful lesson in itself).
DSA: tree recursion, balancing, and the gap between O(log n) and O(n).
7. Min-heap and a task scheduler
BinaryHeap<T> backed by an array, taking a Comparator. Implement insert, extractMin, and heapify.
Apply it: a TaskScheduler where Task has a priority and a deadline, and tasks are processed in priority order.
DSA: sift-up/sift-down, why building a heap is O(n) rather than O(n log n), and the top-K elements problem.
Level 3: Design meets algorithms
8. Your own HashMap<K, V>
Separate chaining with an array of linked lists, a load factor of 0.75, and resizing with rehash.
OOP: the equals/hashCode contract. Create a Person class that breaks the contract and watch lookups fail.
DSA: collision handling, amortized O(1), and why a bad hash function degrades to O(n).
9. LRU Cache (a classic interview problem)
interface Cache<K, V> with get and put. LRUCache must do both in O(1) using a HashMap plus a doubly linked list. Then add an LFUCache implementation of the same interface.
Why it’s valuable: it forces you to combine two data structures inside one well-encapsulated class.
10. Graph library and route planner
interface Graph<V>, with AdjacencyListGraph and AdjacencyMatrixGraph implementations. Algorithms go in a separate GraphAlgorithms class: BFS, DFS, Dijkstra, topological sort, and cycle detection.
Apply it: a city-to-city shortest route planner with weighted edges, and a course-prerequisite scheduler using topological sort.
OOP: separating data representation from algorithms.
DSA: Dijkstra with your own heap from problem 7.
11. Trie-based autocomplete
Trie with insert, search, startsWith, and suggest(prefix, k), which returns the top-k words ranked by frequency.
DSA: prefix trees, DFS with pruning, and combining with a heap for the top-k.
12. Parking lot or library management system
Object-oriented design problems: model ParkingLot, Level, Spot (subclasses CompactSpot, LargeSpot), Vehicle, and Ticket. Find the nearest free spot efficiently, using a priority queue per spot type.
OOP: class relationships (is-a vs has-a), SOLID principles, and enums.
Level 4: Capstones
13. Text editor with undo/redo
Operations are Command objects (InsertCommand, DeleteCommand) with execute() and undo(), managed by two stacks.
OOP: the Command pattern. DSA: stacks. Optionally use a gap buffer or rope for the text itself.
14. Expression evaluator
Parse "3 + 4 * (2 - 1)" with the Shunting-yard algorithm into an expression tree of Node subclasses (NumberNode, BinaryOpNode). Then evaluate it, and pretty-print it using the Visitor pattern.
This one pulls together polymorphism, recursion, stacks, and trees.
How to get the most out of these
Write JUnit tests first for every class. It forces you to think about the interface before the implementation.
State the Big-O of every public method in a Javadoc comment, then verify it with a quick benchmark.
Don’t use java.util collections for the structure you’re building in a given problem. After you’ve built it, compare your version against the JDK’s (e.g., read the source of ArrayList and HashMap). That’s where much of the learning happens.
Refactor as you go: by problem 10, you should be reusing your own heap, list, and map rather than rewriting them.